<interrogationes unitas="xar">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I keep pointers into an array valid while it grows?
    <responsum symbolum="xar_addere xar_obtinere">Use a Xar: it grows by
      adding segments (each twice the size of the last) and never
      reallocates, so a pointer from xar_addere or xar_obtinere stays
      valid for the life of the piscina. New elements are zeroed unless
      the array was created with XAR_VEXILLUM_SINE_ZEPHYRUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I treat a xar as a flat C array (memcpy, qsort, pointer arithmetic)?
    <responsum symbolum="xar_copiare_ad_tabulam xar_ordinare xar_obtinere">No.
      The segments are not contiguous: xar_obtinere(x, 0) points into
      the first segment only. Copy a range out with
      xar_copiare_ad_tabulam, sort in place with xar_ordinare, and
      index every element through xar_obtinere.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I remove an element from the middle of a xar quickly?
    <responsum symbolum="xar_removere_cum_ultimo xar_removere_ultimum xar_tollere">xar_removere_cum_ultimo
      moves the last element into the hole: O(1), but the order is not
      kept. At the end, xar_removere_ultimum drops the last element and
      xar_tollere copies it out first. There is no order-preserving
      removal from the middle.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a xar hold more than about 33 million elements?
    <responsum symbolum="xar_creare_magnum xar_addere">Not the
      ordinary one: its segment table has XXIV entries (about XXXIII M
      elements with the default first segment of IV), and past that
      xar_addere prints a refusal to stderr and returns NIHIL rather
      than truncating silently. Create the array with xar_creare_magnum
      (LXIV segments) when it really is that large.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why must a comparator passed to xar_ordinare not allocate from the array's piscina?
    <responsum symbolum="xar_ordinare piscina_notare piscina_reficere">xar_ordinare
      is a stable merge sort whose scratch space (2n elements) comes
      from the array's own piscina between piscina_notare and
      piscina_reficere. Anything the comparator allocates there is
      rolled back with the scratch when the sort returns. On a
      fixed-size piscina with no room left it falls back to an
      in-place insertion sort (O(n^2), no allocation).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
